      "title": "Researchers evaluate interface designs for multi-minute agent micro-waits",
      "summary": "An arXiv study examines micro-waits during multi-minute AI agent tasks, finding users experience idle frustration when watching progress but suffer attention fragmentation and lost context when navigating away.",
      "design_implication": "A user initiates a complex agent prompt and tabs away to another tool, losing task context before completion. Progress interfaces must shift from passive loading spinners to stateful milestone previews and re-entry summaries that support graceful task resumption.",
      "implication_lens": "pattern",
      "visual_need": "illustrate",
      "source_name": "arXiv cs.HC",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27170",
      "source_date": "2026-10-21",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "claim": "Agent execution creates micro-waits that leave users caught between waiting and task-switching.",
          "quote": "where a single turn routinely runs for minutes. This produces a micro-wait that is too long to watch and too short to leave."
        },
        {
          "claim": "Switching away from running agents harms focus and degrades the context needed to resume.",
          "quote": "switching away fragmented attention and forfeited the context needed to re-"
        }
      ],
      "scores": {
